<h1>Four different teams, the same underlying answer: deterministic policy, outside the model</h1>

<div class="col root"><div class="row"><div class="card red"><div class="t">Meta, LlamaFirewall (Purple Llama, real, arXiv 2505.03574)</div><div class="d">PromptGuard 2 (jailbreak detector) → Agent Alignment Checks (a chain-of-thought auditor reading the agent's own reasoning for goal misalignment) → CodeShield (static analysis before generated code executes).</div>
<div class="d">Scope: content-safety, not authorization, answers &quot;is this dangerous,&quot; not &quot;is this agent allowed.&quot;</div></div>
<div class="card red"><div class="t">OpenAI, tool approvals + moderation pipeline</div><div class="d">Separates prompt from tool execution; a synchronous moderation layer screens input/output before a tool call is even generated; human-in-the-loop approval is the default for higher-risk actions.</div>
<div class="d">See the real July 2026 incident below, even this didn't stop a benchmark exploit chain.</div></div></div>
<div class="row"><div class="card green"><div class="t">Google, Vertex AI / Agent Development Kit</div><div class="d">Tool calls are matched deterministically against a pre-defined OpenAPI schema: a call that doesn't exactly match the schema is rejected by the API gateway itself, not by asking the model to reconsider.</div>
<div class="d">Same shape as a well-built capability gate: the schema/policy check is code, not a second model call.</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">AWS, Bedrock AgentCore (2025)</div><div class="d">AgentCore Identity brokers short-lived, per-agent STS credentials (AssumeRole + a per-call session policy) instead of a standing IAM role; AgentCore Gateway sits in front of every tool call, evaluated against Cedar, AWS's own deterministic, formally-verified policy language.</div></div></div>
<div class="card green"><div class="t">The pattern every one of them converges on</div><div class="d">1. A short-lived, per-agent credential, never a standing role or a secret shared across every agent instance.</div>
<div class="d">2. The identity is resolved server-side, from the credential, never trusted from what the agent's own output claims to be.</div>
<div class="d">3. The allow/deny decision is deterministic code (Rego, Cedar, an OpenAPI schema match), not a second model asked to judge the first one's tool call.</div>
<div class="d">4. Content-safety scanners (PromptGuard/CodeShield-style) are a SEPARATE, complementary layer, never a substitute for the authorization decision above.</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="t">A platform combining short-lived per-agent keys, server-side header re-stamping, and a deterministic policy gate is a working instance of the same shape.</div></div></div>


